<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Portable Windows App Check CLIENT for the OpenBurnBar Windows port
    (Phase 2 · CloudSync · App Check client lane — consumes the Phase-0 mint
    backend functions/src/callables/windowsAppCheck.ts).

    This is the DEPENDENCY-FREE, platform-agnostic half of the App Check feature:
      • Attestation/  — the WindowsAttestationClaim wire mirror, the
        IAttestationProducer seam, the MockAttestationProducer (byte-parity with
        the server's signMockAttestation), and the mock MAC formula.
      • Mint/         — the Firebase-callable wire client (request encode /
        response decode / fail-closed classification) behind an
        IAppCheckMintTransport network seam, plus the endpoint URL resolver and
        the IFirebaseIdTokenSource auth seam.
      • Token/        — the AppCheckToken model (TTL / expiry / refresh math) and
        the IClock seam.
      • Provider/     — WindowsAppCheckProvider: install + TTL-aware refresh +
        single-flight + fail-closed X-Firebase-AppCheck attachment.

    It has NO Windows dependency on purpose — the SAME assembly is:
      • referenced by the net8.0-windows TPM adapter
        (OpenBurnBar.CloudSync.AppCheck.Windows), which supplies the real CNG
        NCryptCreateClaim attestation producer (AC-013 / dev-host-deferred), AND
      • referenced by the net10.0 HttpClient transport
        (OpenBurnBar.CloudSync.AppCheck.Net), AND
      • unit-tested on the macOS authoring host today via `dotnet test`
        (windows/tests/cloudsync-appcheck), exactly the pattern proven by the
        Cast / Home Assistant portable integration libraries.

    Parity source (server): functions/src/callables/windowsAppCheck.ts
      • signMockAttestation                 -> Attestation/MockAttestationMac.cs
      • WindowsAttestationClaim (interface) -> Attestation/WindowsAttestationClaim.cs
      • mintWindowsAppCheckToken (callable) -> Mint/AppCheckMintClient.cs
    Parity source (client lifecycle): AgentLens/Services/OpenBurnBarAppCheckProviderFactory.swift
                                      + AgentLens/Services/AppCheckAttestationMonitor.swift
      -> Provider/WindowsAppCheckProvider.cs

    Targets net8.0 so the net8.0-windows adapter can reference it, while the
    net10.0 test + transport projects consume it too (a lower TFM is consumable by
    a higher one). TreatWarningsAsErrors keeps the port honest.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <RootNamespace>OpenBurnBar.CloudSync.AppCheck</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>OpenBurnBar.CloudSync.AppCheck</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
